WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.083
Avery asks Sam to prepare a one-page booking guide.

2
00:00:03.223 --> 00:00:06.048
Simply saying, handle it, leaves too much unclear.

3
00:00:06.188 --> 00:00:13.155
Delegation means assigning responsibility while making the expected result and decision authority understandable.

4
00:00:13.295 --> 00:00:18.060
Avery remains accountable for ensuring that the work receives appropriate support.

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.711
Our four-part teaching checklist is result, authority, support, and check-in.

6
00:00:22.851 --> 00:00:25.336
The result is a guide patrons can follow.

7
00:00:25.476 --> 00:00:29.034
Sam may reorganize wording and test a draft with colleagues.

8
00:00:29.174 --> 00:00:33.510
Changes to booking eligibility or fees still need Avery's approval.

9
00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.215
These boundaries make independence possible.

10
00:00:36.355 --> 00:00:43.044
Support includes access to current policies, time during the workday, and a person to ask about accessibility.

11
00:00:43.184 --> 00:00:46.504
Avery and Sam agree to review a draft before release.

12
00:00:46.644 --> 00:00:52.596
The check-in is a chance to address obstacles, not a surprise inspection after an unclear assignment.

13
00:00:52.736 --> 00:00:57.213
Micromanaging would mean dictating every sentence and checking constantly.

14
00:00:57.353 --> 00:01:01.391
Abandoning the task would mean providing no help and blaming Sam later.

15
00:01:01.531 --> 00:01:04.380
Good delegation sits between those extremes.

16
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:12.055
The amount of support should fit the task's risk and the employee's experience, with adjustments as confidence and capability grow.
